<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1918</h1><p class="ref-lede">1918 in world history: 96 events, 17 births, 29 deaths, 7 works, 74 states founded and 74 that came to an end. 816 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a><a href="/years/1915">1915</a><a href="/years/1916">1916</a><a href="/years/1917">1917</a><a href="/years/1918" aria-current="page">1918</a><a href="/years/1919">1919</a><a href="/years/1920">1920</a><a href="/years/1921">1921</a><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-4">4 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Soviet Russia recognises Finland's independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Lenin's government confirmed Finnish independence, declared in December 1917. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised the new state the same day, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> soon slid into civil war.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourteen-points.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Woodrow Wilson presents his Fourteen Points</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Addressing Congress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">President Wilson</a> outlined a peace programme including open diplomacy, free seas, self-determination and a league of nations. It framed the armistice and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-6">6 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British women over thirty win the vote</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Representation of the People Act gave the vote to women over 30 who met property qualifications, about 8.4 million women. Equal voting rights with men followed in 1928.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-powers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-3">3 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Soviet Russia signs the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, giving up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Baltic lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, with about a third of the old empire's population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat later voided the treaty.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US adopts standard time zones and daylight saving time</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Standard Time Act gave legal force to time zones first used by the railways and introduced daylight saving time to save fuel. Daylight saving proved unpopular and was repealed in 1919.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/allies" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allies.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-21">21 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Germany launches its Spring Offensive</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Michael struck British lines on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a> after a five-hour bombardment, gaining more ground in days than any attack since 1914. It ran out of steam, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> counter-attacked that summer.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Royal Air Force formed as the first independent air force</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> merged the Royal Flying Corps and the Royal Naval Air Service into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, the world's first air arm independent of army and navy control.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Red Baron is shot down and killed near Amiens</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Manfred von Richthofen, credited with 80 victories, was killed while pursuing an enemy aircraft over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a>. Whether a Canadian pilot or Australian ground gunners fired the fatal shot is still debated.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Azerbaijan and Armenia declare independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">As the short-lived Transcaucasian federation broke up, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> proclaimed independence. Both were absorbed by Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1920.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paris" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US Marines attack at Belleau Wood</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">American Marines launched costly assaults on German positions west of Château-Thierry. Three weeks of fighting helped halt the German drive on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">German ambassador Mirbach is assassinated in Moscow</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a> shot Count Wilhelm von Mirbach in the German embassy, hoping to wreck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a> peace. Their uprising was crushed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s sole ruling party.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Bolsheviks execute the Romanov family in Yekaterinburg</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In the early hours, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>, his wife, their five children and four retainers were shot in the cellar of the Ipatiev House. Their remains were found decades later; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> canonised the family in 2000.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Allied attack at Amiens breaks the German line</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">British, Australian, Canadian and French troops, with hundreds of tanks, broke through near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>. Ludendorff called it the 'black day of the German army'; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a> Offensive had begun.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Corporal Alvin York captures 132 German soldiers</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In the Argonne Forest, Corporal Alvin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> led a small patrol that silenced German machine guns, killing about 25 soldiers and taking 132 prisoners. He received the Medal of Honor and became a national hero.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Armistice of Mudros takes the Ottomans out of the war</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ottoman and British representatives signed an armistice aboard HMS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>. Allied occupation and partition plans followed, provoking the Turkish national movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The poet Wilfred Owen is killed in action</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Wilfred Owen was killed crossing the Sambre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> Canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a week before the armistice. His poems, including 'Dulce et Decorum Est', became the defining English verse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Wilhelm II's abdication is announced as Germany becomes a republic</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With revolution spreading, Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Max von Baden</a> announced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>'s abdication and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a> proclaimed a republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Wilhelm fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> the next day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/allies" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allies.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The armistice ends fighting on the Western Front</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Signed at 5 a.m. in a railway carriage in the forest of Compiègne, the armistice between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> took effect at 11 o'clock. The day is marked as Armistice or Remembrance Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/vienna" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-12">12 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Austria is proclaimed a republic</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A day after Emperor Charles I renounced participation in state affairs, the provisional national assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> proclaimed the Republic of German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule there, dating from 1278, was over.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Charles withdraws from Hungarian affairs</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At Eckartsau, Charles I renounced any part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s government, two days after a similar declaration for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> was effectively at an end.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-18">18 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Latvia proclaims its independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The People's Council in Riga declared an independent Latvian state. After a war of independence it remained free until Soviet occupation in 1940, regaining independence in 1991.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Podgorica Assembly votes for Montenegro's union with Serbia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica-assembly">Podgorica Assembly</a> deposed King Nicholas I and the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and voted for unconditional union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, days before the creation of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. Opponents considered it illegitimate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising">Christmas Uprising</a> followed.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica-assembly">Podgorica Assembly</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Armistice of 11 November 1918 was the agreement that ended fighting on the Western Front between the Allies and Germany in the First World War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/kiel-mutiny" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiel-mutiny.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/kiel-mutiny">Kiel mutiny</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Kiel mutiny was a revolt by sailors of the German High Seas Fleet in 1918, sparked by orders for a final naval sortie against the British at the end of the First World War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-istanbul" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/occupation-of-istanbul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-istanbul">Occupation of Istanbul</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The occupation of Istanbul was the military occupation of the Ottoman capital by Allied forces following the empire's defeat in the First World War, beginning in 1918.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/german-revolution-of-1918-1919" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-revolution-of-1918-1919.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/german-revolution-of-1918-1919">German Revolution of 1918–1919</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The German Revolution of 1918–1919 began with sailors' mutinies in late 1918 and spread across Germany.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villa-giusti" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villa-giusti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villa-giusti">Armistice of Villa Giusti</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Armistice of Villa Giusti was signed on 3 November 1918 at a villa near Padua by Austria-Hungary and Italy on behalf of the Allies, and took effect the next day.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Finnish Civil War was fought from January to May 1918, shortly after Finland's independence, between the socialist Reds, strongest in the industrial south, and the government's Whites under Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/aster-revolution" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aster-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/aster-revolution">Aster Revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Aster Revolution was the uprising in Budapest of 28-31 October 1918, as Austria-Hungary collapsed at the end of the First World War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-lys-1918">Battle of La Lys (1918)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of La Lys was the opening of the German spring offensive in Flanders known as Operation Georgette, launched on 9 April 1918.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad">Battle of Sardarabad</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Sardarabad was fought from 21 to 29 May 1918 west of Erivan, when Armenian forces under generals Movses Silikyan and Daniel Bek-Pirumyan stopped an Ottoman offensive into the Ararat plain.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tampere" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tampere.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tampere">Battle of Tampere</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Tampere was the decisive battle of the Finnish Civil War, fought in March and April 1918.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/danish-icelandic-act-of-union" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danish-icelandic-act-of-union.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/danish-icelandic-act-of-union">Danish–Icelandic Act of Union</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Danish–Icelandic Act of Union was an agreement between Denmark and Iceland that took effect on 1 December 1918, recognising Iceland as a fully sovereign kingdom in personal union with Denmark under King Christian X.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/operation-faustschlag" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/operation-faustschlag.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Campaign</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/operation-faustschlag">Operation Faustschlag</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Faustschlag was the German and Austro-Hungarian offensive on the Eastern Front in February and March 1918, launched after Bolshevik Russia broke off peace talks.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-batum">Treaty of Batum</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Batum was signed on 4 June 1918 between the Ottoman Empire and the new republics of Armenia, Georgia and Azerbaijan after the collapse of the Transcaucasian Federation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hamel" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hamel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hamel">Battle of Hamel</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Hamel was fought on 4 July 1918 near Le Hamel on the Somme in France.</p></div></li></ol></section>
